\begin{abstract}
Vision-language-action (VLA) models built on large vision-language models (VLMs) generalize semantically, but VLM pre-training emphasizes high-level understanding and under-represents low-level spatial cues and physical dynamics. Prediction-oriented pre-training has the opposite profile. We present UP-VLA, a unified understanding and prediction VLA that trains a single autoregressive backbone with a multi-modal understanding (MMU) objective on image-text data and a future visual prediction (PRE) objective on robot video data, combined through unified prompting and attention masking. Actions are generated by a joint prediction-and-understanding mechanism conditioned on a generated scene description and predicted future image tokens. On CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D, UP-VLA reaches an average completed length of 4.08, a 33\% improvement over GR-1 (3.06), and 4.42 on ABCD$\rightarrow$D (GR-1: 4.21). In real-robot experiments it improves both generalization to unseen objects and precise manipulation. Ablations show that both objectives contribute.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Large vision-language models (VLMs) have become a popular initialization for robot policies because they bring open-world semantic knowledge \citep{brohan2023rt,kim24openvla}. However, VLMs are mostly pre-trained on high-level understanding tasks such as visual question answering, and therefore tend to under-emphasize low-level visual and spatial information (depth, size, relative pose) and physical dynamics, which matter for precise manipulation, long-horizon control and adaptation to new scenes. In contrast, policies pre-trained for future frame or video prediction \citep{wu2023unleashing,black2023zero,du2024learning} capture spatial and dynamical structure, but lack the semantic robustness of VLMs (\cref{fig:intro}).

