This creates a dichotomy. VLM-initialized VLAs excel at semantic generalization, e.g., to unseen objects and instructions, but struggle with fine-grained control; prediction-oriented pre-training improves spatial grounding and physical consistency but is weak at semantic robustness (\cref{fig:compare}). Precise manipulation, long-horizon control and adaptation to new scenes require both conceptual and physical competence, which motivates a training paradigm that retains the strengths of both: high-level multi-modal understanding and low-level future prediction.

