We propose UP-VLA, which unifies multi-modal understanding and future prediction in one autoregressive backbone. Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}\setlength\itemsep{0pt}
\item UP-VLA, a unified VLA jointly trained on multi-modal understanding and future prediction in an autoregressive framework.
\item A unified prompting and attention-masking strategy that mixes continuous MMU tokens and discrete predictive tokens in a single LLM.
\item A joint prediction-and-understanding action mechanism conditioning actions on both semantic scene descriptions and predicted futures.
\item State-of-the-art results on CALVIN (ABC$\rightarrow$D average length 4.08, $+33\%$ over GR-1) and real-world gains in semantic generalization and precision tasks.
\end{itemize}

Our key insight is that aligning language-grounded scene comprehension with explicit future image modeling and action generation in one backbone enriches representations with both semantics and low-level spatial and dynamics features. Concretely, we train a single VLA with two complementary objectives, MMU on large-scale image-text data and PRE on robot video and action data, and fuse them with unified prompting and attention masking so that action generation conditions on both semantic scene descriptions and predicted visual futures.

\section{Related Works}
\textbf{VLM-based VLAs.} Language-conditioned manipulation has been studied with pre-trained representations and language-grounded policies \citep{nair2022r3m,shridhar2022cliport,ma2023liv}. Large VLMs have since been adapted into policies, e.g., RT-1/RT-2 and open-source successors \citep{brohan2022rt,brohan2023rt,kim24openvla,li2023vision}, or used for planning and reasoning \citep{huang2022inner,huang2023voxposer,mu2024embodiedgpt}. They inherit semantic generalization, but their pre-training does not emphasize fine spatial or dynamic cues.

\textbf{Prediction-based policies.} Another line pre-trains on future frame or video generation and uses the predicted futures to guide actions \citep{wu2023unleashing,black2023zero,du2024learning}. 3D-VLA \citep{zhen20243d} and 3D Diffuser Actor \citep{ke20243d} bring 3D scene representations into policies. These methods improve spatial grounding but lack semantic knowledge from large-scale image-text data.

\textbf{World models and video pre-training for control.} Predictive models of the future are used both as planners and as pre-training signals \citep{du2023video,du2024learning,wu2023unleashing}. Their predictions give an explicit account of dynamics, but without language-image co-training they inherit little open-world semantics, which is the gap UP-VLA targets.

\textbf{Unified understanding and generation.} Recent multimodal models unify understanding and image generation in a single transformer \citep{xie2024show,sun2024autoregressive,ge2024seed}. UP-VLA builds on this direction for embodied agents, and uses the pre-trained VLM tradition \citep{liu2024visual,radford2021learning} together with predictive pre-training in one model.

\textbf{Why not either alone?} A VLM-initialized policy (the RT-2 style) is trained for next-token prediction on text and discretized actions; the resulting representation is rich in object identity but, as the overview argues, under-emphasizes depth, size and relative pose. A prediction-based policy (the GR-1 style) is trained to forecast future frames and therefore models how the scene changes, but it is trained without large-scale language-image data. Our ablations (below) quantify each side: removing prediction collapses long-horizon CALVIN performance (4.08 to 1.44), while removing MMU leaves CALVIN almost unchanged (3.89) but cuts real-world unseen-object success from 0.58 to 0.20.

\textbf{Related-work positioning.} Methods such as 3D-VLA and PAD-style joint denoising \citep{zhen20243d,guo2024prediction} combine prediction with action generation, whereas UP-VLA additionally retains a language-generation (understanding) pathway and feeds its output back into action generation. Unlike generalist VLAs trained at scale \citep{kim24openvla,black2024pi_0}, we study how a small backbone (Phi-1.5) benefits from combining two complementary pre-training signals.

\section{Preliminaries}
Given the current observation $O_t$ and a language instruction $L$, a VLA policy outputs a chunk of low-level actions $a_{t:t+\Delta t}$. We use a language backbone (Phi-1.5, \citealp{li2023textbooks}) with two visual pathways: a continuous encoder (CLIP-ViT, \citealp{radford2021learning}) with an MLP projection $E_1$ producing continuous image tokens $u$ in the language embedding space, and a discrete VQ-GAN tokenizer \citep{esser2021taming} $E_2$ producing codebook indices $v$ for images. Future prediction operates on token indices, and a VQ decoder reconstructs images. All encoders are frozen; the LLM is fully fine-tuned.

\section{Methodology}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{figures/fig3_v3.png}
\caption{Overview of UP-VLA.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

\textbf{Unified prompting and attention masks.} UP-VLA formats inputs and attention differently per task (\cref{fig:overview,fig:mask}). For \emph{MMU}, the input is $\{[\mathrm{MMU}], u, l\}$ with image tokens before text so that they attend to each other, and the LLM predicts next language tokens: $\hat{L}=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(I,L)$. For \emph{PRE}, the input is $\{[\mathrm{PRE}], l, v_t\}$, with image tokens after text so that they attend to all prior tokens; future image tokens are predicted at aligned positions rather than as a next-token shift: $\hat{O}_{t+\Delta t}=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t,L)$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig4.png}
\caption{Illustration of the unified prompting and attention mechanism.}
\label{fig:mask}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Joint prediction and understanding for action.} Given $(O_t,L)$, we first generate a scene description $s=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(O_t,L_{\mathrm{prompt}})$ (e.g., ``describe this image''), embed additional visual information $E_1(O'_t)$, and form the extended instruction $L'=[E_1(O'_t),s,L]$. The model then jointly predicts future image tokens and action tokens, $(\hat{O}_{t+\Delta t},\hat{H}^A_{t:t+\Delta t})=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t,L')$, so actions attend to both semantic (MMU) and predictive visual tokens. A lightweight policy head, a single-layer attention pooling (MAP) followed by an MLP, maps action tokens to continuous actions: $\hat{a}_{t:t+\Delta t}=\mathrm{MLP}(\mathrm{MAP}(\hat{H}^A_{t:t+\Delta t}))$.

\textbf{Objectives.} The MMU loss is cross-entropy over text tokens, $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MMU}}=-\sum_i \log p_\theta(l_i\mid u,l_{1:i-1})$; the prediction loss is cross-entropy over future image tokens, $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PRE}}=-\sum_{j=1}^{M}\log p_\theta(v'_j\mid l,v_{1:M})$; and the action loss is $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ACT}}=\lVert\hat a_{\mathrm{pos}}-a_{\mathrm{pos}}\rVert_2^2+\mathrm{BCE}(\hat a_{\mathrm{end}},a_{\mathrm{end}})$. The total loss is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}=\lambda_1\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MMU}}+\lambda_2\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PRE}}+\lambda_3\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ACT}}.
\end{equation}

\textbf{Training and inference.} Stage 1 pre-trains on a mixture of MMU data (LLaVA-tuning-665k, \citealp{liu2024visual}) and PRE data (25k Bridge demonstrations, \citealp{walke2023bridgedata}) with task-specific prompts and masks. Stage 2 fine-tunes for actions on downstream robot data with joint prediction-and-understanding prompting, continuing MMU co-training to retain semantic ability. At test time, UP-VLA generates the scene description $s$, forms $L'$, runs the PRE pathway to obtain future tokens and action tokens, decodes actions with MAP+MLP, and repeats autoregressively.

\textbf{Design rationale.} Three choices follow from the above. (i) \emph{Two visual pathways.} Continuous CLIP-ViT features suit understanding, whereas a discrete VQ-GAN codebook allows future images to be predicted with the same cross-entropy loss as text, so one LLM handles all outputs. (ii) \emph{Position of image tokens.} In MMU, images precede text so that image tokens attend to each other and text is generated afterwards; in PRE, images follow the text so that every predicted image token sees the instruction and the current image tokens, and predictions are made at aligned positions instead of a next-token shift. (iii) \emph{Scene-conditioned prompting.} Feeding the self-generated scene description $s$ together with extra visual features $E_1(O'_t)$ into the instruction lets the action tokens read a semantic summary of the scene in addition to the raw predictive tokens.

\textbf{Inference cost.} At test time each control step first decodes a scene description and then runs the PRE pathway for future tokens and actions; this extra sequence length from joint tasks is one of the overheads discussed in the limitations.

\section{Experiments}
\textbf{Setup.} In simulation we use CALVIN \citep{mees2022calvin} in the ABC$\rightarrow$D and ABCD$\rightarrow$D settings, reporting the success rate for completing 1--5 chained tasks and the average completed length. Real-world experiments use a Franka Emika Panda arm with more than 2k demonstrations over 6 skills, evaluating seen tasks, unseen-object grasping and precise operations (e.g., cable routing, small-object grasping), with 20 trials per task (\cref{fig:env}). Baselines are VLA-based (Robo-Flamingo, 3D-VLA, UP-VLA-RT-2, RT-1) and prediction-based (GR-1, SuSIE, Uni-Pi, 3D Diffuser Actor, Diffusion Policy) methods; UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu is a GR-1-like variant with our backbone. The overview does not report optimization hyperparameters, so we do not state them.

