\textbf{Real world.} As shown in \cref{fig:real}, on seen tasks the LLM-backbone methods outperform RT-1 and Diffusion Policy, with UP-VLA competitive or best. For unseen-object grasping, UP-VLA-RT-2 outperforms UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu, while UP-VLA is best overall, indicating semantic gains from MMU and its alignment with actions. On precision tasks the ordering reverses (UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu above UP-VLA-RT-2), and UP-VLA is again best, highlighting the benefit of future prediction for spatial detail.

\textbf{Reading the CALVIN tables.} On ABC$\rightarrow$D the held-out scene D is unseen at training, so the benchmark probes generalization. UP-VLA completes the first task 92.8\% of the time and still completes five in a row 69.9\% of the time, whereas GR-1 falls from 85.4\% to 40.1\% over the same chain and Robo-Flamingo from 82.4\% to 23.5\%. 3D Diffuser Actor has the highest first-task success (93.8\%) but declines to 41.2\% at task five, so UP-VLA's advantage is concentrated in long chains. RT-1 (0.90), Diffusion Policy (0.56), 3D-VLA (0.71) and Uni-Pi (0.92) stay below 1.0, and SuSIE reaches 2.69. The RT-2-style variant of our own model reaches only 1.44, whereas the GR-1-like variant reaches 3.13, so on CALVIN prediction matters more than inheriting VLM weights alone; adding MMU on top lifts the average to 4.08. On ABCD$\rightarrow$D, where all scenes are seen, margins shrink (4.42 vs.\ 4.21 and 4.09) yet UP-VLA is best from the second task onward (92.1\%, 87.9\%, 84.2\%, 81.2\%), while Robo-Flamingo has a marginally higher first-task rate (96.4\% vs.\ 96.2\%). Baseline numbers are copied from the original papers except for starred reproductions.

\textbf{Real-world protocol.} Each task is evaluated with 20 trials. Seen tasks measure in-distribution skill execution; unseen-object grasping tests semantic transfer to objects absent from demonstrations; precise operations such as cable routing and small-object grasping test spatial accuracy. Pooled over these categories, the full model reaches 0.80 on seen and 0.58 on unseen tasks in the ablation table. The per-task numbers are shown graphically in \cref{fig:real}; we do not restate values that are not tabulated in the overview.

\textbf{Ablations.} \Cref{table:ablate} ablates components. Removing prediction drops CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D length from 4.08 to 1.44, showing prediction is crucial for spatial and dynamics grounding; removing Bridge pre-training gives 2.74. Removing MMU barely changes CALVIN (3.89) and seen tasks (0.85) but lowers unseen-object success from 0.58 to 0.20, and removing MMU conditioning gives 3.99 and 0.50, so MMU is critical for semantic generalization and conditioning actions on its scene description helps further.

\textbf{Ablation analysis.} The four ablations isolate different roles. \emph{w/o Prediction} removes the PRE objective and action-time future prediction; CALVIN collapses to 1.44 and real-world seen and unseen success fall to 0.65 and 0.35, so prediction is crucial for spatial and dynamics grounding. \emph{w/o Bridge-Pretrain} keeps the objectives but skips predictive pre-training on 25k Bridge demonstrations; CALVIN falls to 2.74 and unseen success to 0.30, showing that predictive pre-training on a modest amount of robot video transfers downstream. \emph{w/o MMU} keeps CALVIN near the full model (3.89 vs.\ 4.08) and seen tasks even slightly higher (0.85 vs.\ 0.80) but unseen success drops to 0.20. \emph{w/o MMU-Condition} keeps MMU training but does not condition actions on the generated description: CALVIN 3.99, seen 0.80, unseen 0.50. The 0.08 gap on unseen tasks between full and w/o MMU-Condition indicates that explicitly aligning semantics with actions helps beyond co-training alone. With only 20 trials per task and no reported variance, differences of a few points (e.g., seen 0.85 vs.\ 0.80) should not be over-interpreted.

