\textbf{Visualization.} In VQA (\cref{fig:vis}), UP-VLA identifies objects and approximate relative positions, which assists action grounding, with occasional misidentification attributed to backbone and data scale. Predicted futures show robot and object motion consistent with instructions, with background color artifacts in unseen scenes (D vs.\ ABC), suggesting limited generative generalization.

\section{Limitations}
Generative artifacts (background color shifts across scenes) indicate insufficient visual generation pre-training; better tokenizers or diffusion-based modules may help. MMU occasionally misclassifies objects, reflecting backbone and data scale; larger VLMs and richer curated data could reduce this. Joint tasks add training cost and sequence length, which parameter-efficient fine-tuning or structured memory might address. Extending to 3D inputs (depth, point clouds), multiple views or explicit physics modeling could further improve spatial and dynamics understanding. The ablations are single-configuration numbers without reported variance.

\section{Conclusion}
UP-VLA unifies multi-modal understanding and future visual prediction in one autoregressive VLA and conditions actions on both scene descriptions and predicted futures. It achieves state-of-the-art CALVIN results (4.08 on ABC$\rightarrow$D, 4.42 on ABCD$\rightarrow$D) and improves semantic generalization and precision in real-world manipulation, a practical path toward generalist robotic policies with balanced high-level and low-level capabilities.

